    <body>
      <sec id="article-184.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Transcatheter patent foramen ovale (PFO) closure is a minimally invasive procedure used to eliminate a persistent interatrial communication that can allow paradoxical embolization from the venous to the arterial circulation. Although many PFOs remain asymptomatic, closure is commonly performed in carefully selected patients with cryptogenic stroke, transient ischemic attack, or systemic embolism when no alternative cause is identified. The procedure involves advancing a catheter through the femoral venous system into the heart and deploying a septal occluder device across the foramen ovale to seal the defect. Imaging guidance with fluoroscopy and echocardiography is used to ensure proper device positioning. Transcatheter closure has demonstrated effectiveness in reducing recurrent embolic events in appropriately selected patients.</p>
        <p>Clinicians participating in this course gain a comprehensive understanding of the indications, patient selection criteria, diagnostic evaluation, procedural technique, and postprocedural management associated with transcatheter PFO closure. Participants review the role of echocardiographic and catheter-based imaging in identifying PFO anatomy, learn strategies for device selection and deployment, and understand the potential complications and their management. The activity also emphasizes multidisciplinary coordination among cardiologists, imaging specialists, and other healthcare professionals to optimize patient selection, procedural success, and long-term outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Assess patient anatomical suitability and clinical indications for catheter-based patent foramen ovale (PFO) closure and evaluate procedural outcomes, complication risks, and device deployment success in PFO closure cases.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Create a step-by-step procedural plan for performing transcatheter patent foramen ovale closure in various clinical scenarios and improve procedural efficiency and patient safety through optimized catheter and imaging techniques.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Compare different occluder devices and catheter systems used in patent foramen ovale closure procedures.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with an interprofessional team to develop workflow strategies that improve team collaboration, implement safe catheter practices, and evaluate real-time clinical information to optimize patient outcomes during patent foramen ovale closure procedures.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=184&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=184">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Patent foramen ovale (PFO) is a small opening between the 2 atria of the heart (see <bold>Image.</bold> Patent Foramen Ovale) and is among the most common cardiac defects. Results from several large, population-based studies estimated no differences in incidence (at about 9.2%) between patients with&#x000a0;stroke and those without a stroke.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r1">[1]</xref> PFO is present in one-fifth of the population and affects males and females equally. The size grows with age, whereas the prevalence diminishes with increasing age.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;PFO has correlations with both cardiac and extracardiac disorders.&#x000a0;The strongest association seen in the literature is with cryptogenic stroke.</p>
        <p>The PFO provides a conduit for emboli to shunt from right to left.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Most present incidentally on echocardiogram following stroke workup. The contribution of the PFO to the burden of cryptogenic stroke&#x000a0;is well-characterized. Several large, population-based studies' results estimate that transcatheter closure of PFO in addition to medical therapy is superior to medical therapy alone in preventing recurrent cryptogenic stroke; it bears mention that PFO closure in these patients has not demonstrated a reduction in the risk of recurrent transient ischemic attack or all-cause mortality. Studies' results show a higher rate of postoperative atrial fibrillation following PFO closure device placement, the long-term effects of which have yet to be studied.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Data exists showing that PFO closure for cryptogenic stroke can be cost-effective.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;One study's results demonstrated that the effectiveness of PFO closure depends on the device used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r6">[6]</xref> The gold standard for diagnosis of PFO remains a transesophageal echocardiogram with a bubble test after a sustained Valsalva maneuver (see <bold>Video.&#x000a0;</bold>Transesophageal Echocardiogram&#x000a0;of a Patent Foramen Ovale). Once identified,&#x000a0;the structure can be assessed to determine whether treatment is needed; surgical, transcatheter, and medical therapies are available.</p>
        <p>The percutaneous transcatheter approach is becoming more favored, as most patients prefer the less invasive procedure.&#x000a0;Results from early randomized trials evaluating PFO closure (CLOSURE I, PC-Trial, and the initial RESPECT study) did not demonstrate a clear superiority of device closure over medical therapy in patients with PFO and a prior clinically evident paradoxical cerebral embolic event. In contrast, more recent randomized controlled trials, including RESPECT Long-Term, REDUCE, and CLOSE, have consistently shown superior outcomes with PFO closure compared with medical therapy alone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>A PFO is a remnant opening of the fetal foramen ovale. The foramen ovale is a conduit for blood to flow from the right atrium to the left atrium in the fetal state. Usually, the PFO closes at birth, but in about 25% of the population, it does not. Within this subset, a few individuals experience symptoms or sequelae associated with PFO patency. These patients should receive an&#x000a0;evaluation for possible PFO closure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Indications for transcatheter PFO closure:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Strong recommendations (class I)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The Society for Cardiovascular Angiography &#x00026; Interventions (SCAI) recommends PFO closure rather than antiplatelet therapy alone in:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Patients aged 18 to 60 years, with a prior PFO-associated ischemic stroke, after thorough exclusion of alternative stroke etiologies, and&#x000a0;the stroke is considered cryptogenic and likely embolic</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Conditional recommendations (class II)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>SCAI suggests PFO closure in selected situations, after individualized risk&#x02013;benefit assessment:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Patients &#x0003e;60 years with a prior PFO-associated stroke</p></list-item><list-item><p>Patients with thrombophilia and a prior PFO-related stroke, in addition to lifelong anticoagulation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Patients with recurrent stroke despite antiplatelet therapy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Patients with high-risk PFO anatomy, including:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Large right-to-left shunt</p></list-item><list-item><p>Atrial septal aneurysm is defined as an excursion of the atrial septum &#x0003e;10 mm from the plane of the atrial septum into the right or left atrium, or a combined total excursion of 15 mm&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Long tunnel PFO</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>There are also indications&#x000a0;for which SCAI does not recommend routine PFO closure&#x000a0;that include:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Migraine without prior ischemic stroke</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Decompression illness (unless recurrent and refractory)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Platypnea&#x02013;orthodeoxia syndrome (closure may be considered in selected symptomatic patients)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Primary prevention of stroke</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Contraindications of percutaneous transcatheter PFO closure include, but are not limited to, the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Active endocarditis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Active bacteremia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Active fungemia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Current sepsis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intracardiac mass</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intracardiac anatomy precluding safe device delivery&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r6">[6]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>The equipment used for a transcatheter PFO closure:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cardiac catheterization laboratory</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sterile gown, gloves, drapes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Echocardiogram</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fluoroscopy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Selected PFO occluder</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Code cart in the event of an arrhythmia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Balloon-tipped angiographic catheter or pigtail catheter</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Guidewire&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The AMPLATZER PFO Occluder&#x000a0;(18&#x02013;35 mm) (St Jude Medical, St Paul, MN, USA) is the preferred device for percutaneous PFO closure. Several other AMPLATZER-like devices may be used interchangeably with comparable efficacy and safety profiles, including the Occlutech Figulla Flex II (Occlutech International AB, Helsingborg, Sweden), Hyperion PFO Occluder (Comed BV, Bolsward, The Netherlands), and GORE HELEX Occluder (WL Gore &#x00026; Associates, Inc, Flagstaff, AZ, USA). In contrast, non&#x02013;AMPLATZER-like devices have been associated with less favorable outcomes, including higher rates of device-related thrombus formation, embolization, and atrial fibrillation. The AMPLATZER multifenestrated septal occluder-Cribriform comes in 18 to 40 mm sizes. A direct PFO suture&#x02013;based closure system (Noblestitch EL; Nobles Medical Technologies II, Inc) has also been introduced into clinical practice as an alternative to device-based occluders.</p>
        <p>Factors influencing device selection include nickel hypersensitivity, contraindications to antiplatelet therapy, increased risk of infective endocarditis, and the anticipated need for future transseptal access.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;In addition, anatomical characteristics of the PFO, such as a tunnel-type PFO, hypertrophy of the septum secundum, the presence of an atrial septal aneurysm, Eustachian valve or Chiari network, multifenestrated PFOs, or a dilated aortic root, may further guide device choice. In patients with small PFOs without an associated atrial septal aneurysm, a 25-mm cribriform PFO occluder or the GORE HELEX device is generally preferred. Use of devices smaller than 25 mm in adults is unnecessary and may increase the risk of device embolisation.</p>
        <p>For patients with a large atrial septal aneurysm and/or a tunnel diameter of 2 to 4 mm, 30-mm devices are recommended. Devices that are 35 mm should be reserved for cases with marked septal mobility and very large tunnel diameters (&#x0003e;4 mm). In selected patients with a very large tunnel and substantial interatrial shunting, the AMPLATZER Atrial Septal Occluder may be an appropriate option, as its central waist can provide effective closure in anatomies that functionally resemble a small atrial septal defect. In patients with a thickened septum secundum (&#x0003e;1.5 cm), the cribriform device is preferred, as the HELEX device requires a compressed tunnel length of &#x0003c;12 mm.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>The personnel&#x000a0;involved in a transcatheter PFO closure:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Interventional cardiologist trained in PFO closure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgical first assistant</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cardiac nurse</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cardiac cath lab technician</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Preparation for transcatheter closure of a PFO&#x000a0;begins with careful patient selection and diagnostic confirmation. Candidates are typically patients with a history of cryptogenic stroke, transient ischemic attack, or systemic embolism in whom alternative etiologies have been excluded. Initial evaluation includes transthoracic echocardiography with agitated saline contrast (&#x0201c;bubble study&#x0201d;) to detect right-to-left shunting. If the diagnosis remains uncertain or further anatomical detail is required, transesophageal echocardiography&#x000a0;or intracardiac echocardiography is performed to:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Confirm the presence of a PFO</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Assess the size of the defect</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluate septal anatomy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Identify associated features:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Atrial septal aneurysm</p></list-item><list-item><p>Large shunt</p></list-item><list-item><p>Long PFO tunnel
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This may influence device selection and procedural planning. A comprehensive evaluation for other stroke etiologies, including vascular imaging, cardiac rhythm monitoring to exclude atrial fibrillation, and hypercoagulable workup when appropriate, is essential before proceeding with closure.</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Preprocedural preparation also includes optimization of medical therapy and procedural planning. Patients typically undergo baseline laboratory testing, including a complete blood count, renal function tests, and coagulation studies. They are&#x000a0;started on antiplatelet therapy, often aspirin (81 mg) with or without clopidogrel (75 mg), before the procedure, depending on institutional protocol.</p>
        <p>Anticoagulation management should be reviewed and adjusted as necessary. The procedure is usually performed in a cardiac catheterization laboratory or hybrid operating suite under conscious sedation or general anesthesia, depending on whether&#x000a0;transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) guidance is required. Femoral venous access is planned for device delivery.</p>
        <p>Imaging guidance, using&#x000a0;fluoroscopy combined with TEE or intracardiac echocardiography,&#x000a0;is used to position catheters and deploy devices. Prior to device placement, operators must evaluate septal anatomy and confirm the absence of thrombus within the atria or venous system. Adequate patient education and informed consent are critical, including discussion of potential risks such as device embolization, arrhythmias, residual shunt, vascular complications, and rare risk of atrial perforation. Careful preparation ensures optimal procedural success and minimizes complications associated with transcatheter PFO closure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>Transcatheter closure of a PFO begins with vascular access, most commonly through the femoral venous approach, which is considered the safest and most practical route for device delivery. This approach allows the occluder device to be advanced in a vertical trajectory from the inferior right atrium to the superior left atrium. After placement of the femoral venous sheath, systemic anticoagulation is initiated with a 60-U/kg bolus of heparin, followed by additional dosing as needed to maintain an activated clotting time greater than 225 seconds.</p>
        <p>Supplemental oxygen is administered via nasal cannula to achieve hyperoxygenation, which helps mitigate potential complications from air embolism during left atrial catheterization. Initial catheterization may involve advancing a balloon-tipped angiographic catheter or pigtail catheter to perform cineangiography and outline left atrial anatomy. Subsequently, a 6-French multipurpose catheter is advanced over a guidewire into the superior vena cava, then positioned posteriorly and withdrawn toward the fossa ovalis.</p>
        <p>Once the catheter reaches the fossa ovalis, the guidewire is advanced across the PFO into the left superior pulmonary vein, and the multipurpose catheter is advanced over the wire into this vein. Correct positioning is confirmed by hemodynamic measurement of left atrial pressure. Passage of the guidewire into the left atrium may occasionally be challenging; in such cases, an alternative guidewire may be required.</p>
        <p>The multipurpose catheter is then exchanged for a guiding or flexible sheath, which is placed in the left atrium, typically over a straight, tetrafluoroethylene-coated stainless-steel&#x000a0;guidewire. Some operators use a sheathless technique, removing both the multipurpose catheter and the femoral sheath, and attaching an accessory adapter to the guiding catheter to allow continuous saline flushing during advancement into the left atrium. Balloon sizing of the PFO can be performed to estimate tunnel length and assist in device selection. When multiple fenestrations impede catheter advancement across the PFO, a 4- or 5-French catheter may be used to facilitate crossing.</p>
        <p>After appropriate device selection, the occluder device is advanced through the sheath, and the left atrial disc is deployed within the left atrium. The device and sheath are then carefully withdrawn until the left atrial disc apposes the atrial septum. With gentle tension maintained against the septum, the right atrial disc is deployed, sandwiching the interatrial septum between the&#x000a0;2 discs. Contrast injection through the sheath adjacent to the right atrial disc helps confirm proper device positioning. Visualization is typically performed in the left anterior oblique projection, where the spacing between the discs corresponds to the thickness of the septum secundum. Following deployment and release of the device, right atrial angiography may be performed to confirm correct placement and device function.</p>
        <p>Imaging guidance during PFO closure may include fluoroscopy alone or in combination with intracardiac echocardiography or TEE. Evidence remains inconclusive regarding the superiority of one modality over another. Some studies' results suggest that TTE guidance can be effective, potentially shortening procedural time while eliminating the need for general anesthesia and endotracheal intubation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r9">[9]</xref> In contrast, reliance on fluoroscopy alone may increase procedural time and cost when balloon sizing is required.</p>
        <p>Following the procedure, TTE&#x000a0;with an agitated saline bubble study is performed to confirm device positioning and evaluate for residual shunting. An echocardiogram and chest radiograph are commonly obtained to assess for procedural complications and confirm device placement. Anticoagulation or antiplatelet therapy is generally continued for approximately 6&#x000a0;months after device implantation. Dental procedures should ideally be postponed during this period; if necessary, antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended to reduce the risk of infective endocarditis. Routine follow-up&#x000a0;TTE&#x000a0;at 1 and 6 months&#x000a0;is recommended to ensure device stability and confirm complete PFO closure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Data show that complication rates for transcatheter PFO closure are approximately 7%. Some studies' results have shown that the incidence of adverse events is higher in patients older than 60 years of age. Furthermore, adverse outcomes are higher in patients with a preceding ischemic stroke versus a transient ischemic stroke. Complications include the following in order of most common to least:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Arrhythmia (most commonly atrial fibrillation)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Vascular complications</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemorrhage/hematoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cardiac tamponade/perforation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Loss of life</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pneumothorax/hemothorax</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Occluder embolus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infection</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>PFO closure is not necessary for all patients. However, a select number of patients benefit significantly from PFO closure. The sequelae in this population are many times more devastating, especially in those with debilitating strokes. Therefore, primary care clinicians must recognize the indications for PFO closure and refer patients appropriately. Knowing that&#x000a0;PFOs greater than 25 mm are usually not amenable to percutaneous management and more often require surgical closure is critical information to share with the patient.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-184.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;In addition to indications and contraindications for PFO closure, every practitioner should be aware of&#x000a0;the common postoperative complications; arrhythmia is the most common, but death is also a possibility.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Effective transcatheter PFO closure requires coordinated care among an interprofessional team to ensure appropriate patient selection, procedural safety, and optimal outcomes. Physicians and advanced practitioners must develop strong skills in identifying patients who may benefit from closure, particularly those with cryptogenic stroke after thorough exclusion of other etiologies. Strategic evaluation involves collaboration with neurology, cardiology, and imaging specialists to interpret echocardiographic findings, assess PFO anatomy, and determine procedural candidacy. During the procedure, cardiologists and catheterization laboratory staff rely on precise imaging guidance and technical expertise to ensure accurate device deployment. Nurses play a critical role in preprocedural preparation, patient monitoring, and postprocedural recovery, while pharmacists contribute by optimizing antiplatelet or anticoagulation therapy and monitoring for potential drug interactions or complications.</p>
        <p>Strong interprofessional communication and care coordination are essential for maintaining patient safety and improving clinical outcomes. Neurologists, cardiologists, advanced practitioners, and imaging specialists must communicate clearly when evaluating stroke etiology and determining whether PFO closure is appropriate. Nurses provide ongoing monitoring for complications such as arrhythmias, vascular access issues, or device-related problems, and they play an important role in patient education regarding medication adherence and activity restrictions after the procedure.</p>
        <p>Further, pharmacists assist in managing antithrombotic regimens and ensuring appropriate prophylaxis when indicated. Structured follow-up with echocardiography and coordinated outpatient care helps confirm successful device positioning and identify residual shunting or late complications. This collaborative, patient-centered approach enhances team performance, reduces procedural risk, and supports long-term prevention of recurrent embolic events.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-184.s13">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=184&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=184">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
